<!--
    The world map's per-unit blip templates, transcribed from the reference's own
    `Interface\FrameXML\WorldMapFrameTemplates.xml` (l.3-35). Two virtual Frames and nothing else:
    `WorldMapUnitTemplate` (a party member) and `WorldMapRaidUnitTemplate` (a raid member), which
    inherits it and re-states its OnLoad without the `party` token.

    **Why they are a file of their own rather than four hand-written copies inside WorldMapFrame.xml.**
    They used to be exactly that: `WorldMapParty1..4` written out four times, each carrying an
    ANONYMOUS `<Texture>`. Anonymous is the bug. The reference's blip names its icon `$parentIcon`,
    and that published name is load-bearing for addons — pfUI's `modules/mapcolors.lua` recolours
    party and raid blips by class and reaches them as

        frame.icon = _G[frame_name.."Icon"]
        frame.icon:SetTexture(pfUI.media["img:circleparty"])

    so against four anonymous textures line 11 raised `attempt to index field 'icon' (a nil value)`
    and took pfUI's whole module chain down with it. A template gets the naming right once.

    **The raid pool is DECLARED but not DRIVEN, and that is the honest half-step.** `mapcolors`
    loops `1, MAX_RAID_MEMBERS` (40) as well as `1, MAX_PARTY_MEMBERS` (4), so all 44 frames have to
    exist for it to survive; the reference declares all 44 too (WorldMapFrame.xml l.486-529). What
    ours cannot do yet is POSITION the raid forty: `GetPlayerMapPosition` resolves `player` and
    `party1..4` and answers (0,0) for every `raid` token (worldmap.rs l.487-505), and the
    reference's second raid arm — battleground team members — needs `GetNumBattlefieldPositions`
    and `GetBattlefieldPosition`, neither of which we bind. So the forty are declared, hidden, and
    left where the reference leaves them until a raid actually has coordinates to place them at.
    Declaring a frame the reference declares is not stubbing behaviour (1134 §4): the frames and
    their art are real, and the missing piece is a data source, named here and in WorldMapFrame.xml's
    OUT list rather than faked with an empty setter.

    The hover is the reference's `WorldMapUnit_OnEnter`, which reads `this` and owns
    `WorldMapTooltip` — both of which now exist, so the template's `WorldMapTooltip:Hide()` OnLeave
    has something to hide. Our body is the single-unit case; the reference additionally walks every
    overlapping blip with MouseIsOver and stacks their names into one tooltip, which we do not.
-->
<Ui xmlns="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/ui/">
    <!-- No enableMouse, exactly as the reference declares it: the OnEnter/OnLeave below
         AUTO-ENABLE the mouse on their own (wow-re ui/scratch/scripts-auto-enable.md §1,
         VERIFIED — a script handler reaches the same enable primitive 0x76af00(2,-1) the
         attribute does). Adding the attribute would be redundant, and frame_flag_gate reads
         the loaded ENGINE rather than our XML precisely so the two spellings cannot drift. -->
    <Frame name="WorldMapUnitTemplate" hidden="true" virtual="true">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="16" y="16"/></Size>
        <Anchors><Anchor point="CENTER"/></Anchors>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                <Texture name="$parentIcon" setAllPoints="true"/>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>
                this:SetFrameLevel(this:GetFrameLevel() + 1);
                this.unit = "party" .. this:GetID();
                getglobal(this:GetName() .. "Icon"):SetTexture("Interface\\WorldMap\\WorldMapPartyIcon");
            </OnLoad>
            <OnEnter>WorldMapUnit_OnEnter()</OnEnter>
            <OnLeave>WorldMapTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

    <!-- Ref l.29-35 verbatim: the same frame WITHOUT the `party` token, because a raid blip's unit
         is assigned by the positioning pass (`partyMemberFrame.unit = unit`, WorldMapFrame.lua
         l.386) rather than derived from its id. -->
    <Frame name="WorldMapRaidUnitTemplate" inherits="WorldMapUnitTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>
                this:SetFrameLevel(this:GetFrameLevel() + 1);
                getglobal(this:GetName() .. "Icon"):SetTexture("Interface\\WorldMap\\WorldMapPartyIcon");
            </OnLoad>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>
</Ui>
